# Messages the file mailer writes in development. Correspondence, and not
# something to commit. Both paths are anchored with a leading slash, naming the
# two places the mailer can put them: the web application's content root, which
# is where a relative Mail:Directory resolves, and the repository root for
# somebody running from there.
#
# Anchored because a bare "mail/" is what was here and it matched far more than
# it was meant to. Git compares ignore patterns without regard to case wherever
# core.ignoreCase is on, which it is on every Windows checkout, so "mail/" also
# swallowed src/JiranisokoTech.Application/Mail,
# src/JiranisokoTech.Infrastructure/Mail and tests/JiranisokoTech.Tests/Mail —
# IMailer, Letters, every notification this system sends and the eleven tests
# that cover them were invisible to git, and so to every review of every commit
# that referred to them. The commit that added the candidate letters committed the
# twelve lines that register them and nothing else. Master does not build from a
# clean clone: the compiler asks for a namespace no clone has ever had, and the
# suite is eleven tests shorter than the one anybody ran. Nothing warns about any
# of it, because the files are simply not there and git calls the tree clean.
/mail/
/src/JiranisokoTech.Web/mail/

# Candidate CVs written by the file store. Documents belonging to people
# outside this firm, and not something to commit. Anchored for the reason above:
# "cvs/" would have taken a source folder called Cvs with it the day somebody
# added one.
/cvs/
/src/JiranisokoTech.Web/cvs/

# Worktrees the agent tooling creates. Not part of the project.
.claude/worktrees/
